RFC 3161 timestamp-token fixtures (tests/fixtures/evidence_tsa)

Origin: self-generated. Every key, certificate and token in this directory was made by
gen.sh in this directory with OpenSSL 3.0.13 on 2026-10-12, for testing only. No real
timestamp authority, certification authority or third-party data is involved. The
certificate names ("Kshana Test Root RSA", "Kshana Test TSA P-256", ...) are invented.
The private keys were created in a temporary directory and deleted; they are not here.

Licence: the same as the repository (AGPL-3.0-only; see LICENSE and LICENSING.md).

Contents
  data.txt, other.txt     the message that was stamped, and a different one
  certs/*.pem             test trust anchors and certificates (public certificates only)
  tokens/*.tsr            TimeStampResp as written by `openssl ts -reply`
  tokens/*.tst            bare TimeStampToken (CMS ContentInfo) written by `openssl cms -sign -cades`
  cases.tsv               the comparison cases (token, data, trust anchors, untrusted certs)
  openssl-verdicts.txt    the oracle's verdict per case, written by run_openssl.sh
  gen.sh, run_openssl.sh  how the fixtures and the oracle verdicts were made

Transforms: the *-sigflip, *-tstinfo-changed, *-intsigflip and *-ess-twin tokens are copies of
good tokens with one byte changed or one embedded certificate replaced, as gen.sh describes.
Regenerating with gen.sh produces different bytes (new keys and times); regenerate
openssl-verdicts.txt with run_openssl.sh at the same time.

Not vendored: tokens from any public timestamp authority. freetsa.org publishes no licence
or terms of use for its tokens or certificates (checked at https://freetsa.org/index_en.php
on 2026-10-12), so its token is fetched at test time by an ignored test instead
(tests/evidence_tsa_openssl.rs, freetsa_token_agrees_with_openssl).
